# Changelog on Push

Before running `git push`, update the changelog in `src/data/changelogs.ts`.

## When to update

Update the changelog whenever a push includes user-facing changes (features, fixes, UI, docs shown in-app). Skip only for purely internal or tooling-only commits with no user-visible effect.

## Style (product release notes, not a commit diary)

- Prefer **one release entry per ship**, especially for same-day work.
- Typically **3–8 bullets**. Bundle related fixes; omit reverts, refactors, and tiny polish users will not notice.
- User-facing language only.
- Do **not** recreate micro-patch history (`v1.3.xxx` spam). Historical notes were consolidated into era releases; keep the list short going forward.

## SemVer

Follow [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/):

| Bump | Example | When |
|------|---------|------|
| **MAJOR** | `v2.0.0` | Breaking user-facing changes |
| **MINOR** | `v1.5.0` | New features or a meaningful bundled improvement set |
| **PATCH** | `v1.4.1` | Bug fixes and small polish |

After a MINOR (`v1.4.0`), patches are `v1.4.1`, `v1.4.2`, … until the next feature bundle → `v1.5.0`.

## How to update

Add a **new object at the top** of the `changelogs` array in `src/data/changelogs.ts`:

```typescript
{
  version: 'v1.4.1',
  subtitle: 'Short Title',
  date: 'July 29, 2026',
  changes: [
    { type: 'Fixed', text: 'User-facing fix in plain language' },
    { type: 'Improved', text: 'User-facing improvement in plain language' },
  ],
},
```

Categories (Keep a Changelog style): `Added` | `Changed` | `Fixed` | `Improved` | `Security`.

Always use an exact date like `July 9, 2026`, never `Early/Mid/Late-Month Year`.

## Workflow

1. Finish code changes and commit them.
2. Add the changelog entry summarizing what shipped (correct SemVer bump).
3. Commit the changelog update (same commit or a follow-up `chore: update changelog` commit).
4. Push to GitHub.

Never push code changes without a matching changelog entry in the same push.
